WEBVTT
TITLE Type Checking And Type Systems — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A type checker asks one question at every operation: are these values of a kind this operation accepts?

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
It runs after parsing and before intermediate code: it needs the syntax tree, and it hands on an annotated tree.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
GATE CSE 2018 asked which statement was false. "Type checking is done before parsing" was the false one.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
GATE CSE 1998 asked when type checking is normally done. The answer is during semantic analysis.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A type expression is a small tree: a base type, or a constructor applied to type expressions.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
array(10, int) says ten elements, each an int. pointer(char) says a pointer to a char.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
A function type maps a product of parameter types to a result: (int x float) to bool.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
A type expression is not a syntax tree. The tree says how the program is written; the type says what the values may be.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Structural equivalence: same constructor, and recursively equivalent components.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The record tag is not part of the comparison, so point and vec with the same fields are one type.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The field name is part of the comparison, because the product carries name and type for each field.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
A recursive type makes the comparison a graph walk, so visited pairs are marked to stop it looping.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Name equivalence: two types are equal only if they carry the same name.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
On the same nine declarations, structure gives five classes and name gives seven, and the page counts both.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
A typedef is not a new type. It is another name for an existing one, under both schemes.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
C uses structural equivalence except for struct and union, where the tag is the identity.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Static checking happens before execution; dynamic checking happens during execution, using a tag.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Strong typing is a different question: it asks whether type errors are always caught.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Two questions give four boxes: Java static and strong, C static and weak, Python dynamic and strong.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The definition of strong varies by author, so read the definition a question gives you.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A type system is a set of rules of the form: if these premises hold, this expression has this type.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The rule for addition: both operands numeric, and the result is the wider of the two.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The rule for if: the condition must be bool, and both branches must have compatible types.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
A program is well typed when every node has a rule that fires. The node with no firing rule is the error.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Compatibility is not equality: identical, assignable and coercible are three different relations.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Coercion is a conversion the compiler inserts; a cast is one the programmer writes.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
On this three-type lattice, strict assignment allows three of nine pairs and coercing assignment allows four.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Overloading is resolved by applicability, then exactness. Two candidates that both need a widening is an ambiguity.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A simple type checker is a syntax-directed definition with one attribute, type, on every expression.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Leaves take their type from the symbol table; each operator node applies its rule to its children types.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
A failing rule sets the error type, and error poisons its parents, so one mistake gives one message.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Without the poison the same six-node tree reports four messages instead of one.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Statements need their own rules: a condition must be boolean, and a loop body must be a statement.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
A name must be declared before use, and a call must match arity and parameter types.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
GATE CSE 2021 Set 1 asked what one SDT could type-check: it handled the integer part and not the boolean part.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
GATE CSE 2026 Set 1 compared two SDDs for declarations: same language, same entries, one S-attributed.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Static checking is sound but incomplete: it never lets a guaranteed failure through, and it rejects some programs that would run.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step one: build the type expression for every declaration and store it with the name.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step two: walk the tree bottom-up, applying one rule per node, with error as a type and not an exception.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step three reports the failing node and checks statements. Step four keeps equivalence and compatibility apart.

